Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||07-09-2000||08:48 PM||||Oreif,  I'm still a little confused on the pistons. I have to get new ones no matter what as the cylinders are getting bored. But I've heard that when you switch to the 3.1 crank/connecting rods you need to use the 3.1 piston or the correct width at a shorter heigh to make sure everything clears. I'm using the stock '88 2.8 heads, my mind is a little foggy right now so I can't remember if their alum or cast. I would like to retain the aprx stock compression just so I don't have to pay for high grade gas tho  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ah well, thanks for the help again guys.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||07-09-2000||09:33 PM||||The 3.1L heads are mostly aluminum and the 2.8L are cast. The pistons for the cast heads on a 3.1 need to be dished for clearence. Compression is at 8.8 to 1.<BR>The aluminum heads use the flat top pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric||07-09-2000||09:42 PM||||Oreif, where do you get your facts?  The 2.8 uses the same rods as the 3.1 and the 3.4.  The 3.1 uses a bigger crank, identical to the 3.4, and the same block as the 2.8.  The 3.4 has a beefier block which has a larger bore.  The pistons are different for all engine sizes. ||||reg||11
Z||000012||Oreif||07-09-2000||10:24 PM||||The 3.1L stroke is longer. I have the 86 Fiero service manual and one for an 89 Grand Prix. The rods are different lenghts between 3.1 and 2.8. (yes the crank is bigger also) Bore sizes between the 2.8 and 3.1 are the same but the 3.1 uses an aluminum head. (Don't know if 3.1 ever came with cast heads but the 88/89 GP used aluminum heads).  I was told that the 3.1 with the Fiero cast heads requires different pistons. On Silovites piston guide it specifically calls for a different piston when using cast heads. The 3.4 uses the 3.1L crank and rods but piston bore is bigger. In fact the 3.4L crate engine from GM has the main caps stamped "3.1L" I have spent many hours researching and comparing the 2 service manuals because this is what I plan on doing within the next year. A friend has a 3.1 out of an 89 Grand Prix (source of the service manual) and will be delivering the engine in August. I can do the 3.1L swap for less than $1000.00 and get to about 175-180 for horsepower. If I had the money I'd go to a 3.4L. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||07-09-2000||10:39 PM||||Oreif usually has his guano together with this kind of info, but I admit that I'm having trouble following everything he said too. I think he has a couple facts turned around. I pulled out my Chevrolet Power book to double check a couple thing.<P>All 2.8/3.1/3.4 rods are the same<BR>All 2.8/3.1/3.4 pistons are different<BR>The 2.8 crank is different from the 3.1/3.4<BR>The 2.8/3.1 block is the same, except for 84 and older 2.8's (smaller crank journals).<P>There are two 3.1 engines, one with aluminum heads and one with cast iron heads. Engines with cast iron heads were used in many early 90's FWD minivans. Most 3.1 engines have the aluminum heads which have different heads, intake manifold, and pistons.<P>Fiero manifolds will not fit on the aluminum heads. 3.1 engines with aluminum heads have dished pistons to match the heads. If you exchange the aluminum heads on this engine for cast iron ones, the compression ratio will be super low and doggy. If you put the aluminum heads on an engine meant for cast iron heads, the valves might hit the pistons.<P>Rickguy, sounds like you're trying to decide whether to rebuild the Fiero 2.8 or stroke it to 3.1. If that's the case, the main things you need are a 3.1 crank, neutrally balanced flywheel from a 3.1, and a set of 3.1 pistons in the correct oversize to match your bore. The Fiero Store and others sell a stroker kit if that's easier for you. BTW, all Fieros had cast iron heads from the factory.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||07-09-2000||10:54 PM||||FOCOA did a three part story on stroking a 2.8 to a 3.1.  It was very detailed.  I used the article when I replaced my 2.8 with a Grooms 2.8 longblock because it had good info on porting the heads, intake, and exhaust.  You can get back issues from FOCOA.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||3
Z||000015||Oreif||07-10-2000||09:56 AM||||Batboy, Everything you stated is the same as I stated except the rods. I was only stating the bore size of the 2.8 and 3.1 was the same size. Pistons are different lengths. Between the 2 service manuals I show about a 1/16" difference. Unless I am reading the measurement wrong. Is the stroke of the cranks about 1/16" different? The service manuals are at work. I'll check out exactly what they are calling the measurement. It could be I am confusing stroke with rod length.<P> As for what I plan on doing with the 3.1L I am getting, I am going to use the 3.1L block crank, rods, Flywheel, and fuel injectors. I am going to install the silovite #1486 pistons (for use with cast heads) The 2.8L cast heads, intake, and all other external 2.8L parts. Crane cams valve train and cam. <BR>I know I need to use the 3.1L parts for the crank because the 3.1 is internally balanced where my 86 2.8L is externally balanced.<P>If any of the above on what I plan on doing sounds wrong please let me know. <BR>Here is a link to the Silovite pistons.<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||ThatRickGuy||07-10-2000||12:14 PM||||(acidental dual post)<p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 07-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||ThatRickGuy||07-10-2000||12:14 PM||||Okay, just got off the phone with the shop. Dian was looking threw her parts list and this is what we came up with:<P>2.8/3.1 same block<BR>3.4 thicker cylinderwalls/bigger bore<P>2.8/3.1 Same pistons (assuming cast heads)<BR>3.4 larger pistons<P>2.8-3.4 same rods<P>2.8 wussy torqueless crank<BR>3.1-3.4 much better crank<P>There are 2 different cranks for the 3.1 tho, a distributer and a magnetic. I'm assuming that the distrubuter styled crank is the appropriate one for our 2.8s.<P>As for the pistons, well, I'll have to get over sized so the cylinder walls can get cleaned up. But as far as dishing, well, I'll tlak to Dianne about compression.<P>The only other thing I'm worried about (well okay 2) 1. The harmonic balancer, is the stock 2.8 enough, or do I need to find the 3.1? Also, the flywheel err flex plate, what ever the automatic version of the big gear is called, Someone said that I needed the 3.1, but I've heard that the 2.8 nuetrally balanced will work also. Any idea? <P>Unfortunatly I'm going on vacation at the end of this week so I can't drop the engine off until I get back, but I'm planning to have this job finished befor it gets cold out again. (always waiting on fundage) I'll keep ya posted as I get things going.<P>oh yeah, a reconditioned 3.1 crank and bearings runs $197 + $20 core (non-refundable)||||reg||1
Z||000018||ThatRickGuy||07-11-2000||07:59 AM||||Just when you think you know what you're going to do...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.tacreationsusa.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tacreationsusa.com</A> <P>They have used 3.4 engines going for $350-450, 1 mo warrenty, extended available. Even with the extra mounting work that's much cheaper then the 3.1 stroker/rebuild.||||reg||1
