Z||000020||Phil||07-11-2000||01:08 PM||||To add more confusion- rods are the same (2.8  3.1) the  wrist pin holes are in a different place on the 3.1 pistons. So you can reuse the rods but definately need new pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000021||fiero56||07-11-2000||02:39 PM||||I just got off the phone with t/a creations. The guy on the phone was helpful, answered all my questions, and was knowledgeable about what he had. I didn't tell him what I planned to put the 3.4 in, and he didn't ask, so no confrontation. They have 4 3.4s for sale right now, all for $400 plus shipping, +-$90. They come with everything(heads, oil pan, intake, etc) except accessories(a/c, ps, alternator, etc). Might be able to offset the cost of the engine by selling off the parts I don't need. I'm pretty sure I know where I'm getting my next engine. I had to hold myself back from ordering one from him on the spot. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000022||DiggityDan||07-11-2000||02:55 PM||||As a side note about the TA creations, there's a place in Dallas, TX called Bishop automotive that sells all of their engines as cores. I called them to get prices on some blocks, 2.8&3.1 longblocks and 3.4 shortblocks where around $150-250. The only way you can buy a core from them is if you have the casting number on the block. I don't believe there is any guarantee as far as what you are going to end up with except that it will be rebuildable.||||reg||1
Z||000023||batboy||07-11-2000||07:35 PM||||Thatrickguy, I don't know who told you that the pistons for both the 2.8 and the 3.1 are the same, but they most certainly are NOT interchangeable. The bore (diameter) is the same, but like Oreif said, the wrist pin placement is different. Think about it. If the rods are the same length for both 2.8 and 3.1, but the 3.1 has a longer stroke, then if you use 2.8 pistons with a 3.1 crank, the piston will poke up higher than the deck at top dead center, which will hit the head and/or valves for sure. Yes, a neutrally balanced flex plate/flywheel from a late model 2.8 will also work (maybe 88 and newer?). I just figured it was simpler to find a bone yard 3.1 and then rob the crank and flywheel off it. I'm not sure about the balancer, I'd probably get it too just to be safe. Maybe somebody else knows for sure.<P>Oreif, yeah, you were right on everything but the rods. Just a couple things were hard for me to follow so I was only trying to clarify a little bit. Peace. The 2.8 stroke is 2.99" (76mm) and the 3.1/3.4 stroke is 3.31" (84mm).||||reg||1
Z||000024||rogergarrison||07-11-2000||09:11 PM||||Jasper Engines will sell a total rebuilt long block for 2.8 or 3.1 starting at $1300.00. they will even custom build to your specification, cam, piston, rods, rockers, ect for more. They even give you an unlimited milage warranty.||||reg||1
